WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.399
Retrieval-based language models sharpen their
predictions by pulling examples from a huge

00:00:05.479 --> 00:00:07.369
external datastore at test time,

00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:12.232
but that nearest-neighbor search is slow and runs
at almost every single token.

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:16.110
This paper introduces RetoMaton, short for
retrieval automaton,

00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:20.111
which builds a weighted finite automaton on top of
the datastore.

00:00:20.191 --> 00:00:25.159
By saving pointers between consecutive entries and
clustering entries into states,

00:00:25.239 --> 00:00:30.946
RetoMaton lets the model follow cheap automaton
transitions instead of searching from scratch.

00:00:31.026 --> 00:00:36.978
The result: up to eighty-three percent fewer
nearest-neighbor searches with no loss in perplexity,

00:00:37.058 --> 00:00:41.780
or up to one point eight five lower perplexity when
the search budget is kept.
